The following companies provide their observations based on their own simulation results. [DCM] Observation 1: The handover rate of UAV UEs in some cases (e.g., 50m) is higher than that of the terrestrial UEs. Observation 2: The handover failure rate of UAV UEs in some cases (e.g., 50m with 30km/h, 300m with 160km/h) is higher than that of the terrestrial UEs. Observation 3: The ping-pong rate of UAV UEs is lower than that of the terrestrial UEs. Based on our observations, it is proposed that RAN2 should study further the mobility enhancements for UAV UEs to improve the handover performance. [Huawei] Observation 1: The handover rate of the drone UE is nearly 2 times higher than that of the terrestrial UE. Then the trend of simulation results indicated that the UE height has a significant impact on the HO performance, and the drone UE suffers higher HO rate than the terrestrial UE. Observation 2: The HOF rate of the drone UE is at most 17 times higher than that of the terrestrial UE. Then the trend of simulation results indicated that the UE height has a significant impact on the HO performance, and the drone UE suffers much higher HOF rate than the terrestrial UE. Observation 3: The RLF rate of the drone UE is higher than that of the terrestrial UE. Then the trend of simulation results indicated that the UE height has a significant impact on the RLF performance, and the drone UE suffers much higher RLF rate than the terrestrial UE. Observation 4: The time in handoff of the drone UE is higher than that of the terrestrial UE. Then the trend of simulation results indicated that the drone UE experiences much longer time in handoff than the terrestrial UE. Observation 5: The time in Qout of the drone UE is higher than that of the terrestrial UE. Then the trend of simulation results indicated that the drone UE experiences much longer time in Qout than the terrestrial UE. Observation 6: The Ping-Pong rate of the drone UE is slightly higher than that of the terrestrial UE. 